Write a Python function that solves the shortest-path problem in a three-dimensional grid.

The signature is exactly:

    def solve(grid, start, goal):

Arguments:
- grid is a three-dimensional list grid[x][y][z] of size 5 x 5 x 5;
  the value -1 means an impassable obstacle, 0 means a free cell;
- start is the [x, y, z] list of the start cell;
- goal is the [x, y, z] list of the goal cell.

Movement rules: Movement is allowed to a neighboring cell along six directions (along the X, Y, Z axes, one step at a time). Each step costs 1. Return the length of the shortest path in steps.

If no path exists, return -1. The start and goal cells are always passable.

IMPORTANT: you do not see the maps themselves — your function will be run on hidden maps generated separately. Therefore solve the problem in its general form; do not fit it to an example.

Give the answer as one code block opening with exactly ```python and closing with exactly ```. The block must contain only the definition of the solve function and, if needed, imports. No code outside the function, print nothing, do not call input().

